{
  "dom_id": "step-q338486",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225100/assets/2593d513ce130090a84cc26e71bf321960712a6ccb2d8ff82e55e4728cc0657c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Determine the exact values of the $x$-coordinates of points $A$ and $D,$ in radians.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives the equation $2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining the solutions and ordering them from smallest to largest, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n$\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n$\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
